```kotlin
enum class MessageState { PENDING, SENT, DELIVERED, READ, FAILED }

fun applyStateUpdate(current: MessageState, incoming: MessageState): MessageState {
    val order = listOf(PENDING, SENT, DELIVERED, READ)
    val currentIdx = order.indexOf(current)
    val incomingIdx = order.indexOf(incoming)

    return when {
        incoming == MessageState.FAILED -> incoming
        incomingIdx > currentIdx -> incoming      // only ever move forward
        else -> current                            // ignore out-of-order regressions
    }
}
```

This is the concrete implementation of the state-machine discipline from the receipts slide: an incoming state update is applied only if it's strictly *later* in the sequence than the message's current state, so a `delivered` event arriving after a `read` event (a real possibility given network reordering) doesn't visibly regress the UI's checkmark backward.
